Why it's visionary: Alzheimer's affects 55M people worldwide. If 40 Hz gamma works (trials ongoing), the first verified, free, open-source 40 Hz generator becomes the default tool. The verification is the trust layer that clinics need.
3. The Adaptive Entrainment Loop
Research finding: Brain.fm's advantage isn't the binaural beat itself — it's that the audio adapts to your brain state in real-time via EEG feedback. The Brian Intensify paper (2025) describes ML-driven adaptive auditory stimulation. Nobody has built this in open source.
Expansion: The closed-loop system.
- Input: consumer EEG headband (Muse, OpenBCI, Neurosky — all have Bluetooth APIs)
- SoundWorld monitors alpha/beta/gamma ratios in real-time
- If beta drops below threshold → increase beat drive intensity
- If gamma plateau reached → sustain, don't push
- If user falls asleep (delta dominates) → shift to sleep protocol
- All adaptation is logged with timestamps for post-session review
Architecture:
EEG Headband → Bluetooth → Python bridge → WebSocket → SoundWorld LiveEngine
                                                              ↓
                                                    Adjusts beat_hz, drive,
                                                    pad tension, tempo in real-time
Why it's visionary: This is Brain.fm's core IP, done in open source. The research papers describe exactly this architecture. Nobody's shipped it because consumer EEG was too noisy — but Muse 2/3 and OpenBCI are good enough now for coarse state detection.
